      "text": "First, let's find the side length of the square. Since the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we have the relationship:\n\\[\n\\text{diagonal} = \\text{side length} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nSolving for the side length, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{side length} = \\frac{\\text{diagonal}}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nNext, let's find the area of the square. Since the side length squared is:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of the square is 12 square inches.\nNow, let's find the radius of the circle. Since the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius is half of that:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{2}\n\\]\nFinally, let's find the area of the circle. Since the area of a circle is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\pi r^2\n\\]\nwe have:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of circle} = \\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\cdot \\frac{25}{4} = \\frac{25\\pi}{4}\n\\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{25\\pi}{4} \\approx 19.6\n\\]\nSo the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by:\n\\[\n19.6 - 12 = 7.6\n\\]\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7.6}\n\\]",
