      "text": "Step 1: Find the length of the side of the square.\nSince the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the side of the square. Let s be the length of the side of the square. Then,\ns^2 + s^2 = 5^2\n2s^2 = 25\ns^2 = 12.5\ns = sqrt(12.5) = 5(sqrt(1/2))\nStep 2: Find the area of the square.\nThe area of the square is given by the formula A = s^2. Plugging in the value of s, we get,\nA = (5(sqrt(1/2)))^2 = 25/2 ≈ 12.5 square inches.\nStep 3: Find the radius of the circle.\nThe diameter of the circle is 5 inches, so the radius is half of that, which is 2.5 inches.\nStep 4: Find the area of the circle.\nThe area of the circle is given by the formula A = pi*r^2. Plugging in the value of r, we get,\nA = pi*(2.5)^2 ≈ 19.63455 square inches.\nStep 5: Calculate the difference in area between the circle and the square.\nThe difference in area is given by the formula A_circle - A_square. Plugging in the values we found, we get,\nA_circle - A_square = 19.63455 - 12.5 ≈ 7.1 square inches.\nThus, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by approximately 7.1 square inches.",
