      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to calculate the area of the square and the area of the circle, and then find the difference between these two areas.\n\nFirst, let's determine the side length of the square. We know that the diagonal of the square is 5 inches. For a square with side length \\( s \\), the diagonal \\( d \\) can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[ d = s\\sqrt{2} \\]\nGiven that the diagonal is 5 inches, we can set up the equation:\n\\[ 5 = s\\sqrt{2} \\]\nTo solve for \\( s \\), we divide both sides by \\( \\sqrt{2} \\):\n\\[ s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\]\nTo rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\( \\sqrt{2} \\):\n\\[ s = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the square. The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of a square is given by \\( s^2 \\):\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = \\frac{25}{2} \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = 12.5 \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the area of the circle. The diameter of the circle is 5 inches, and the radius \\( r \\) is half of the diameter:\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of a circle is given by \\( \\pi r^2 \\):\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi (2.5)^2 \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\]\nUsing the approximation \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14159 \\):\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 3.14159 \\cdot 6.25 \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 19.63495 \\]\n\nFinally, we find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Difference} \\approx 19.63495 - 12.5 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Difference} \\approx 7.13495 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} \\approx 7.1 \\]\n\nThus, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
