      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "## Step-by-step Solution:\n1. **Diagonal of the Square**:\n\n   Given that the diagonal of a square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem for a right triangle formed by two sides of the square and the diagonal.\n\n   - The diagonal of a square with side length \\( s \\) is given by \\( s\\sqrt{2} \\):\n     \\[\n     s\\sqrt{2} = 5\n     \\]\n\n   - Solving for \\( s \\):\n     \\[\n     s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Area of the Square**:\n\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\times 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5\n   \\]\n\n3. **Diameter of the Circle**:\n\n   Given that the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius \\( r \\) is half of the diameter:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5\n   \\]\n\n4. **Area of the Circle**:\n\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\times 6.25 \\approx 20 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Difference in Areas**:\n\n   The difference in the areas of the circle and the square is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} = 20 - 12.5 = 7.5 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than that of the square by \\( \\boxed{7.5} \\) square inches.",
